# Implementation Plan — Mesh Peer Tracking via RSSI (30s Auto-Accept Protocol)

Enable peer-to-peer tracking of any device on the ZeroGrid mesh using **Signal Strength (RSSI)** and **GPS coordinates** (if available). The system implements a privacy-first consent workflow where the target has **30 seconds to deny** the tracking request; if no action is taken within 30 seconds, the request is **automatically accepted**, and the requester receives a live RSSI proximity radar and directional guidance.

---

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **1. Direct vs. Multi-Hop RSSI Tracking**:
>
> - **Direct 1-Hop (BLE / Wi-Fi Direct)**: When the target is within physical radio range (~30–80m), the requester's antenna measures raw RSSI (dBm) directly on every received beacon. This powers the high-precision **"Hot/Cold" metal detector** mode.
> - **Multi-Hop Relay**: If the target is several hops away, the request and auto-acceptance still succeed across the mesh. Once accepted, the target's beacons stream their current GPS coordinates across hops, allowing macro-navigation (compass) until the rescuer/responder closes the distance into direct radio range for micro RSSI tracking.

> [!WARNING]
> **2. 30-Second Auto-Accept Privacy Model**:
> When a device receives a `TRACK_REQUEST`, it displays an urgent full-screen or dialog alert with an audible alert chime and a visible 30-second countdown.
>
> - Tapping **[DENY]** immediately cancels the request and notifies the requester.
> - Tapping **[ACCEPT]** starts tracking immediately without waiting.
> - If **30 seconds elapse without interaction** (e.g. user incapacitated, lost phone, injured), the request is automatically accepted and the device begins emitting high-frequency tracking beacons.

